#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060907 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two vectors \n$\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle,$ shown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$  and  $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}?$\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that $\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =(2i+3j)+(-4i+4j) \\\\  & =(2+(-4))i+(3+4)j \\\\  & =-2i+7j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-2} \\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}7}\\mathbf{j} =  \\langle {\\color{red}-2}, {\\color{blue}7} \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}7}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a},$ we multiply each of the components by $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot a & =-5(2i+3j) \\\\  & =(-5\\cdot 2)i+(-5\\cdot 3)j \\\\  & =-10i+-15j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n$$\n-5\\mathbf{a} =  {\\color{red}-10} \\mathbf{i}+ {\\color{blue}-15} \\mathbf{j} = \\langle {\\color{red}-10}, {\\color{blue}-15} \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 10} \\\\ {\\color{blue}- 15}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "84c864c1-dd6f-5d3a-96de-fba662bed5ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2914 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060908 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2,5⟩+⟨-3,7⟩ &= ⟨2+(-3),5+7⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨-1,12⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+(-3) \\\\ 5+7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot ⟨2,5⟩ &= ⟨(-5)\\cdot 2,(-5)\\cdot 5⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨-10,-25⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25665086-90db-5c8c-b344-69f6f45aac54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2915 :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060909 :tutorial/content "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v},$ or\n\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}.$\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}.\n$$\n\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v},$ is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." :tutorial/id #uuid "25d66dd7-21a2-5d10-a19d-333e279d6e7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2916 :tutorial/title "Common Notations Used When Describing Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060922 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ={a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k \\\\ b & ={b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf b$ can be calculated using the following determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \n\t\\left|\n\t\t{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right|\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we have the two vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,-1,5 \\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -1,4,2 \\rangle$. We can find the cross product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 4)i-(2\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-22i-9j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-22,-9,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "675e437a-1553-5dd8-be56-9c002c7f98ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2948 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060923 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula to compute the cross product of two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's find out.\n\nSuppose we want to find the cross product $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a}=a_1\\mathbf{i}+a_2\\mathbf{j}+a_3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=b_1\\mathbf{i}+b_2\\mathbf{j}+b_3\\mathbf{k}.$ Using properties of the cross product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\times ({b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})i\\times i+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})i\\times j+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})i\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})j\\times i+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})j\\times j+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})j\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})k\\times i+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})k\\times j+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})k\\times k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cross product of two parallel (collinear) vectors is equal to $\\mathbf 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}\\times\\mathbf{i} = \\mathbf{j}\\times\\mathbf{j} = \\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, using the definition of the cross product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\times j=k, & \\,j\\times i=-k, \\\\ j\\times k=i, & \\,k\\times j=-i, \\\\ k\\times i=j, & \\,i\\times k=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{i\\times i}_{=0}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{i\\times j}_{=k}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{i\\times k}_{=-j} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{j\\times i}_{=-k}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{j\\times j}_{=0}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{j\\times k}_{=i} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{k\\times i}_{=j}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{k\\times j}_{=-i}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{k\\times k}_{=0} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is equal to the formula given for the cross product of two general vectors using the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "d88205f2-c2c5-581f-bf0a-dfe6aa3f0a70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2950 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula for the Cross Product Using the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060931 :tutorial/content "The **determinant** of a general $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis denoted $\\text {det} ({A})$ and computed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text {det} ({A}) = \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{vmatrix} =  {\\color{blue}ad} - {\\color{red}bc}.\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{1}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nthe determinant is\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} - {\\color{red}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{5}} = 1.\n$$\n\nTo remember how to compute the determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, you can imagine taking the product of the diagonal of the matrix, and then subtracting the product of the off-diagonal terms, as follows:\n\n![](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\nLater, we will see that the determinant is related to the \"volume\" spanned by the row vectors or column vectors in a matrix. We will also see that the determinant can tell us about many other properties of a matrix. \n\nHowever, in this lesson, we will just focus on computing the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "e436044e-13ed-56ec-aac0-2ed10a498cf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3012 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a4550c-7ed7-58aa-8928-23dd1cfd0cd7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725826} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175} {:db/